WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:27.390
The Pentagon Papers, the Iraq War Logs…

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.990
US history is full of infamous leaks that
blew the government wide open.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:37.810
But there’s one set of leaks that stands
head and shoulders above the others.

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:42.250
In 2013, a contractor with the National Security
Agency, or NSA, quietly handed a cache of

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:47.120
documents over to The Guardian, detailing
how the NSA had been hoovering up information

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.240
on millions of Americans while lying to Congress.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.310
It was the single most explosive story of
the post 9/11-era.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.290
The man behind it all?

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:57.290
Edward Snowden.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:01.100
A young computer whizz from North Carolina,
Snowden is today infamous.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.250
Currently holed up in asylum in Russia, he’s
both loved and loathed by millions.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:11.000
To some, he’s a fearless whistleblower who
sacrificed everything to do what was right.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.140
To others, he’s the 21st Century version
of a Cold War defector.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.970
But the truth is more complex than either
of those portrayals.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:24.320
Snowden’s life is one of contradictions,
surprises, and enough shades of gray to fill

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.590
a million E.L.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:26.590
James novels.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:31.590
Today, we investigate the man behind the most
notorious leak in American history.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:40.520
Early Years – The Drop Out
If you know the name Elizabeth City, North

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.159
Carolina, it’s likely because of the Coast
Guard base.

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:47.940
By some measures the busiest in the US, the
base dominates the lives of local residents.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.010
The Snowdens were no exception.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:56.450
When Edward Joseph Snowden was born on June
21, 1983, it was into a family with deep military

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:57.450
connections.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:01.890
Young Snowden’s maternal grandfather, Edward
J. Barrett, was a rear admiral, and his father

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.320
Lonnie also worked in the Coast Guard.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:08.229
For Snowden, that meant a childhood instilled
with deep respect for the services.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:14.130
But it was also a childhood marked by something
more tragic: the slow breakdown of his parents’

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:15.130
marriage.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:19.220
Lonnie and Elizabeth Snowden (known to her
friends as Wendy) were high school sweethearts

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.290
who’d married young.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:23.340
Although Wendy suffered from epilepsy, it
wasn’t debilitating.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:28.819
She managed to get a job as a Maryland district
court clerk, moving the family north in 1992.

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:34.099
But if the Snowdens were expecting their new
lives in Maryland to be easy, they were mistaken.

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:38.710
As Wendy and Lon’s marriage drifted, young
Snowden was stumbling into a crisis at high

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:39.710
school.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:43.510
Despite being clearly intelligent, the teenage
boy didn’t excel at anything.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:47.840
When he missed four months study after catching
glandular fever in 1998, it knocked him back

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.170
to a place he couldn’t recover from.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:51.459
He flunked high school.

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:55.409
Perhaps desperate to salvage something from
this debacle, Snowden enrolled in computer

00:02:55.409 --> 00:02:57.909
classes at Anne Arundel community college.

00:02:57.909 --> 00:03:00.230
But his prospects were low.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:04.830
Message board posts he left around this time
are tinged with cheerful desperation about

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.100
being unemployed.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.630
The family stumbled on for another couple
of years, but by 2001, the writing was on

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:11.630
the wall.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:15.830
Wendy and Lon split, and Wendy took off for
Ellicott City, near Baltimore.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:18.659
One year later, 19-year old Snowden followed
her.

00:03:18.659 --> 00:03:23.080
At this stage, Edward Snowden was about as
far from a potential Biographics subject as

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.330
you’re likely to get.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.920
His only achievement was being webmaster of
an anime message board.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.140
Yet even now, there were two clues to his
future notoriety.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:35.220
The first was his incomparable tech skills.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:39.430
After enrolling at Anne Arundel, Snowden had
discovered he had not just an aptitude for

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:40.430
computers, but a genius.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:41.440
The second was his location.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.310
Ellicott City is home to somewhere that’s
going to become very important to our story.

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:48.920
It’s home to NSA Headquarters.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:52.650
Unfortunately, real life isn’t as narratively
pleasing as fiction, and we need to take a

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.800
quick detour to get there.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.220
On March 20, 2003, the US invaded Iraq.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:03.350
Although the main fighting was over by the
end of May, a call for recruits went out nationwide.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:06.189
This call struck a chord with the unemployed
Snowden.

00:04:06.189 --> 00:04:11.349
Maybe it was his family’s Coast Guard background,
but for whatever reason, Snowden felt compelled

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:12.349
to join up.

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:16.160
In May, 2004, he reported for duty at Fort
Benning, Georgia.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.000
His training didn’t last long.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.019
Snowden later said he broke both legs.

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:22.120
The Army claimed he flunked out with shin
splints.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.280
Either way, the result was the same.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.280
After barely a month, Snowden was discharged.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.590
He returned to Maryland, an unemployed failure.

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:34.710
Finally, in 2005, Snowden got a security guard
job at Maryland's Centre for Advanced Study

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:35.710
of Language.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:40.620
And there his story could have ended, were
it not for one little fact.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.310
The Center was backed by the NSA.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:47.370
And the NSA was desperate to recruit computer
geeks.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:56.370
A World of Shadows
After the Soviet Union fell in 1991, America’s

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:01.190
intelligence agencies went through an era
of downsizing and shedding staff.

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:04.260
That all changed on September 11, 2001.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.830
Faced with the new terrorist threat, the agencies
were desperate to make up for lost time.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:11.640
They were also desperate to catch up with
evolving tech.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:17.090
Way into the 2000s, the senior staff were
so technologically illiterate that some could

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:21.200
barely distinguish a laptop from a sandwich.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.340
It was clearly time to start hiring the best
tech guys.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:28.650
And one of the best just happened to be working
as an NSA security guard.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.650
Within months, Snowden had switched his security
job for IT.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.450
He must have made an impression.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:38.530
Not two years after flunking basic military
training, in mid-2006, young Snowden got a

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:41.440
job offer from the CIA.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.300
Saying “yes” would turn out to be a key
decision in his life.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:49.130
The young IT genius was whisked away to Geneva,
Switzerland in 2007.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:52.740
While his actual job was a mixture of the
cool and the not-so-cool – between maintaining

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:56.960
network security, he was also tasked with
fixing the aircon – the money it paid was

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:58.350
no joke.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:02.960
As a CIA technician, Snowden was able to live
every boy’s James Bond fantasies.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.030
He bought a brand new BMW, befriended an Estonian
rock star, and played the stock market, boasting

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:10.710
he’d lost $20,000 in a single month.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:15.570
He also traveled extensively, washing up in
Rome, in Bosnia, in London.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:18.660
Not that his opinions of these places were
always PC.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.730
Of London’s vibrant Muslim community, he
wrote they were “terrifying….

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:24.130
I didn’t want to get out of the car.”

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:27.370
Yet even in this dream job, disillusionment
could creep in.

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:32.180
In 2008, Snowden supposedly witnessed the
CIA arranging for a banker to be caught drink

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:36.460
driving, then offering to suppress the charge
in return for cooperation.

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:39.150
The Swiss authorities have denied this ever
happened.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:43.470
Still, the anguish Snowden felt working for
the CIA was real enough.

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:47.020
He would later claim:
"Much of what I saw in Geneva really disillusioned

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.430
me about how my government functions...

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:54.080
I realized that I was part of something that
was doing far more harm than good."

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.760
Not that he seemed like a potential whistleblower
just yet.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.470
When the New York Times published a leaked
Pentagon plan to attack Iran in January 2009,

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:06.881
Snowden raged online that anyone who leaked
such information to the press “should be

00:07:06.881 --> 00:07:08.590
shot in the balls.”

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:12.460
Still, there’s no doubt that Snowden was
struggling with something in Geneva, and it

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:13.460
was making him angry.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:17.120
In February, 2009, he abruptly resigned from
the CIA.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.880
Anonymous sources in the Agency later said
he’d been on the verge of being fired for

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:22.690
trying to hack their database.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:25.960
Back in the US, the young computer whizz underwent
a change.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:30.370
His message board posts became full of angry
rants against government programs like social

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:33.740
welfare, and invective against Obama’s new
administration.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:38.400
In a world full of angry young men, it seemed
Edward Snowden was simply another voice screaming

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:39.400
into the online void.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:41.690
But there was one crucial difference.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.170
Snowden was still useful.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:46.880
Within a couple of months of leaving the CIA,
Snowden had a new job, this time as a contractor

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.319
for Dell working on NSA projects.

00:07:49.319 --> 00:07:53.340
The first was working on anti cyberspying
software in Tokyo.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:57.750
For the lifelong anime fan, moving to Japan
should have been a dream come true.

00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:01.320
Instead, it plunged Snowden into a moral nightmare.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:10.770
Secret Plots and “Spooky Types”
On April 5, 2010, Wikileaks’ Julian Assange

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:14.490
previewed a leaked military video at a National
Press Club event.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:18.710
Known as “Collateral Murder” it showed
US military pilots gunning down two Reuters

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:22.090
journalists mistaken for terrorists in Baghdad
in 2007.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:26.160
The video was the first major scoop in the
history of Wikileaks.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.360
It was also the beginning of the biggest intelligence
leak in history.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:33.649
Throughout the rest of the year, Wikileaks
and The Guardian, New York Times, and Der

00:08:33.649 --> 00:08:39.899
Spiegel published the Afghan War Logs, the
Iraq War Logs, and finally a trove of leaked

00:08:39.899 --> 00:08:41.770
US diplomatic cables.

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:45.870
Supplied to Wikileaks by intelligence analyst
Chelsea Manning, the classified files turned

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:49.550
the world’s opinion of the US upside down.

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:50.960
But not Edward Snowden’s.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:55.590
Over in Japan, the young IT professional had
become increasingly disturbed by the work

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:56.900
the NSA was up to.

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:59.940
On February 18, 2010, he’d posted on a message
board:

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:04.990
“It really concerns me how little this sort
of corporate behavior bothers those outside

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:06.870
of technology circles.

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:11.580
Society really seems to have developed an
unquestioning obedience towards spooky types.”

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:15.650
But it wasn’t until Manning’s leaks shook
the media that Snowden seems to have decided

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:16.650
to reveal the NSA’s secrets.

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:21.220
In 2011, Dell transferred him back to Maryland
from Japan.

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:23.510
On the outside, this was perfect for Snowden.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.920
He moved in with his younger girlfriend, the
free spirited Lindsay Mills.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:27.920
But inside?

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.880
Inside, Snowden had already made his decision.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.000
It was while back in Maryland that he started
downloading the documents he would soon release

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:35.620
into the world.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:40.920
In early 2012, Snowden contacted the Tor Project,
an online community dedicated to providing

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.550
everyone with the tools to encrypt their internet
activity.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:48.840
While this seems innocuous enough, Tor was
deeply connected with Wikileaks.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.140
Many have suggested Snowden made contact with
Tor as a way of getting to Assange.

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:58.090
If that was the plan, though, it soon ran
into trouble with a capital T.

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:03.720
On June 19, 2012, the Wikileaks founder fled
to the Ecuadorian embassy in London to avoid

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.400
arrest on a rape charge.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:09.390
The subsequent media circus may have made
Snowden rethink Wikileaks as the place to

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:10.900
publish his revelations.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:16.070
By now, Snowden and Mills were in Hawaii,
where Snowden had work with the NSA’s regional

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:17.500
cryptological center.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:21.330
At this point, he was actively looking for
opportunities to steal documents.

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:26.390
But he may have also been looking for something
else: validation for his genius.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:30.520
That summer, Snowden hacked the answers for
an NSA job promotion test and subsequently

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:31.520
aced it.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.820
When he was offered a new position, he got
angry that it wasn’t high up enough and

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:35.820
rejected it.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:40.490
The NSA’s Deputy Director would later tell
reporters he considered this the moment Snowden

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:42.180
turned on the agency.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:47.650
Whatever the truth, it wasn’t only at work
that people were noticing the change in Snowden.

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:51.910
In July 2012, Lindsay Mills blogged that the
couple’s relationship in Hawaii was under

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:52.910
stress.

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:57.110
By October, she was writing about “heavy
conversations” and “stress paralysis”.

00:10:57.110 --> 00:11:01.650
Whatever was preying on Snowden’s mind was
tormenting them both.

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:05.470
That Thanksgiving, Mills returned to the US
mainland to see her family for an extended

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:06.470
break.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:09.550
While she was away, Snowden finally set the
wheels of his plan in motion.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:15.010
On December 1, 2012, Guardian columnist and
vocal Wikileaks defender Glenn Greenwald got

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:21.650
an unusual email, suggesting he set up PGP
encryption software on his laptop.

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:25.590
Like anyone getting such a paranoid email,
Greenwald ignored it, assuming the sender

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:26.620
was a crank.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:31.720
Little did Greenwald know this was his first
contact with the man who’d soon make his

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:32.720
career.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:39.900
The Heist of the Century
A month after being frozen out by Greenwald,

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:41.100
Snowden tried again.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:45.400
This time, he contacted the journalist’s
friend, documentary maker Laura Poitras.

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:49.160
Poitras was deeply connected to the Tor Project
we talked about earlier.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:53.050
When she first heard from Snowden, she assumed
he was trying to entrap her.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:56.810
But when her contacts at Tor gave him the
green light, she began to listen.

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:00.760
Using a fake name, Snowden told Poitras he
was a high-ranking intelligence officer.

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:03.240
This was a total lie, but the next part wasn’t.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:08.460
He told her he could get hold of files that
would paint the US in a very bad light.

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:12.920
If Poitras wanted to see them, all she had
to do was get Greenwald onboard.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:14.820
That February, Snowden changed jobs again.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:18.770
While still working at the NSA’s Hawaii
center, he took a pay cut to move from contracting

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:21.260
with Dell to Booz Allen Hamilton.

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:26.510
His new role was a Sysadmin, a role with two
unbelievable bonuses for the potential leaker.

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:30.300
One, that it made Snowden one of the only
people at the NSA with a legitimate reason

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.490
to carry normally-forbidden thumb drives.

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:36.200
And two, that it gave him access to almost
the entire system.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:40.370
Still, some to this day are skeptical that
even a Sysadmin could have accessed so many

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:42.210
confidential areas without help.

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:46.470
It’s thought Snowden may have stolen 14
people’s login credentials to maximize his

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:47.500
reach.

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:52.029
Pro-Snowden sources say he used his Sysadmin
title to trick people into handing them over.

00:12:52.029 --> 00:12:56.250
Anti-Snowden sources say he may have received
help from Russian intelligence.

00:12:56.250 --> 00:13:00.760
Back in the real world, Poitras was desperately
trying to convince Greenwald her source was

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:01.940
on the level.

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:05.180
That March, they tentatively agreed to meet
Snowden face to face.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:08.110
Yet the trio missed their first chance that
very month.

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.710
On March 30, Snowden flew back to the US mainland.

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:14.360
But rather than go meet the journalists, he
went to see his father.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:17.440
On April 4, 2013, Lonnie and Edward had dinner.

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:21.940
Lonnie later said his son seemed preoccupied,
like something was weighing him down.

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:23.800
When they finished eating, the two men hugged.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:26.190
“I love you, dad,” said Snowden.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:28.980
“I love you too, son,” Lonnie replied.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:33.150
Back in Hawaii, the disconnect between Snowden’s
private and public lives was growing.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:37.720
At the same time he and Mills moved into a
new house, Glenn Greenwald was receiving two

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:41.180
thumb drives with instructions on how to encrypt
his communications.

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:44.880
Not that Greenwald was the only journalist
Snowden was courting.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:49.350
Barton Gellman of the Washington Post was
also in contact with Snowden in May 2013,

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:52.650
and was even given the first major cache of
NSA files.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:55.060
But Gellman wanted too much time for fact
checking.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:57.950
Angry at the delay, Snowden cut him off.

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:02.640
Between his secret chats with Greenwald, Gellman
and Poitras, Snowden was laying the groundwork

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:03.720
for his escape.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:07.980
He told his employers he was suffering from
epilepsy, just like his mother, and needed

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:09.070
some time off.

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:12.690
On May 17, Mills left for a sailing vacation
with friends.

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:17.870
With remarkable understatement, Snowden told
her he might not be around when she got back.

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:21.880
Three days later, the 29 year old traveled
to Honolulu airport and got on a flight to

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:22.880
Hong Kong.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:27.290
He had with him four laptops and a stack of
thumb drives carrying an estimated 1.7 million

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:28.800
top secret documents.

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:32.680
About a week later, Greenwald got a message
over his newly-installed encrypted system,

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:33.970
asking him to come to Hong Kong.

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:36.150
Not unreasonably, Greenwald asked why.

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:39.250
In response, Snowden sent him a welcome package.

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:41.800
As Greenwald opened the files, his eyes went
wide.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:46.670
Here, in black and white, was solid proof
that the NSA had knowingly lied to Congress

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:49.740
about the extent of its covert surveillance
programs.

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:51.710
And this was just the taster.

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:54.990
The main course would be waiting for him in
Hong Kong.

00:14:54.990 --> 00:14:56.540
Provided he was willing to come.

00:14:56.540 --> 00:15:01.180
Three days later, on June 1, 2013, Greenwald
was on a plane heading for Asia.

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:06.040
Alongside him were Laura Poitras and veteran
Guardian reporter Ewen MacAskill.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:16.900
The story of the century had begun.

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:30.300
Who Watches the Watchmen?

00:15:30.300 --> 00:16:15.000
Much later, when

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:35.610
the dust had settled, Glenn Greenwald would
write that he was expecting to meet a veteran

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:39.410
of the intelligence services in the hyper-crowded
city.

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:45.670
Someone middle aged, with the grizzled look
and gray hair of a longtime secret operative.

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:47.700
In Snowden, he got exactly the opposite.

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:52.330
Prior to leaving, the reporters had arranged
to meet Snowden in the lobby of the Mira hotel,

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:55.730
an imposing glass edifice in trendy Kowloon.

00:16:55.730 --> 00:16:58.970
Snowden had supplied them with code words
and said he would be carrying a Rubik’s

00:16:58.970 --> 00:16:59.970
cube.

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:03.520
The first day, Greenwald and Poitras waited,
only for Snowden to not show.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:08.169
The second day, they waited again, keeping
their eyes peeled for the heavyset ex-military

00:17:08.169 --> 00:17:09.980
man carrying his Rubik’s cube.

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:15.169
When they saw skinny, delicate Edward Snowden,
Greenwald later said his heart sank.

00:17:15.169 --> 00:17:16.990
Still, they stuck to the script.

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:19.159
Greenwald sauntered up to the young man and
asked:

00:17:19.159 --> 00:17:20.850
“What time does the restaurant open?”

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:25.209
“At noon,” came the reply, “but don’t
go there, the food sucks.”

00:17:25.209 --> 00:17:27.540
For a moment, the two stood there awkwardly.

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:31.110
Then Snowden muttered “follow me,” and
led them to his room.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:35.450
Right up until Snowden began talking, Greenwald
was convinced the whole thing was a waste

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:36.450
of time.

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:40.840
But as they settled into the anonymous room
and Snowden began to spin his tale, the doubts

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:42.020
slowly melted away.

00:17:42.020 --> 00:17:45.470
What was left was a feeling of sheer terror.

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:49.520
As Poitras filmed, Snowden told Greenwald
how the NSA could hack any phone in the world

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:52.550
and turn it into a microphone that spied on
its owner.

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:56.810
He explained that nearly every social media
platform had been compromised, that the US

00:17:56.810 --> 00:18:01.251
government – and its allies in Britain,
Israel, and elsewhere – was hoovering up

00:18:01.251 --> 00:18:06.850
metadata on every citizen that gave them an
accurate picture of your entire life.

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:11.570
Your loves, your dreams, your frustrations,
your desires, even the type of sleazy movies

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:15.230
you liked to watch, all of it was available
to access.

00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:17.850
Your privacy was just an illusion.

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:21.300
At some point, Greenwald invited Ewan MacAskill
up to meet Snowden.

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:25.500
As the most experienced reporter there, MacAskill
would be able to sniff out if Snowden was

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:26.500
faking it.

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:29.090
He even had a special code to use with the
Guardian offices.

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:33.790
If Snowden was a hoaxer, he’d text a single
sentence to the US editor: “the Guinness

00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:34.790
is bad.”

00:18:34.790 --> 00:18:39.700
Not long after, a text arrived at the paper’s
offices: “the Guinness is good.”

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:45.510
Greenwald and MacAskill’s first story went
live at 7pm, June 6, 2013.

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:50.100
Detailing how phone company Verizon had allowed
the NSA to collect data on millions of Americans,

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:51.500
it was a bombshell.

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:57.170
Barely 24 hours later, it was followed by
Bart Gellman’s delayed Washington Post story

00:18:57.170 --> 00:18:58.720
on the NSA’s PRISIM system.

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:01.910
In NSA headquarters, the stories triggered
a meltdown.

00:19:01.910 --> 00:19:05.450
They knew they had a mole in their midst,
but who?

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:10.020
On June 8, the agency used digital footprints
to identify Snowden as the likely leaker.

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:14.110
It was the moment the young hacker’s normal
life ended.

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:16.580
To this day, the extent of Snowden’s theft
remains unknown.

00:19:16.580 --> 00:19:22.470
The NSA estimates 1.7 million documents went
missing, of which only one percent have been

00:19:22.470 --> 00:19:23.470
released.

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:25.520
Some think the rest found their way to the
Kremlin.

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:29.360
Others believe Snowden wasn’t lying when
he said he took no copies to Russia.

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:34.870
Like so much in this tale of lies and shadows,
the truth remains hidden.

00:19:34.870 --> 00:19:38.340
But on that day in 2013, the NSA feared the
worst.

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:43.970
At 3pm on June 9, Snowden consented to the
Guardian uploading a video in which he confessed

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:45.250
to being the source of the leaks.

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:49.520
He claimed he’d outed himself to control
the narrative, to stop the NSA spinning a

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:51.530
false counter story.

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:55.280
Former NSA analysts have said it’s because
he’s an egomaniac.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:58.440
Before an hour was out, the video was playing
all over the world.

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:02.890
By the time morning dawned on June 10, a wily
Twitter user had identified the Mira from

00:20:02.890 --> 00:20:03.910
its lamps.

00:20:03.910 --> 00:20:08.870
As journalists converged on the hotel, Snowden
calmly dressed and slipped out into the sprawling

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:10.540
concrete jungle of Hong Kong.

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:15.640
It was a sweltering day, the air thick with
humidity, and Edward Snowden was now the most

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:23.910
wanted man alive.

00:20:23.910 --> 00:20:26.270
The Fugitive
The ripples that slowly spread out from Snowden’s

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:30.179
vanishing act were many, affecting people
half a planet away.

00:20:30.179 --> 00:20:34.520
In Hawaii, Lindsay Mills panicked and pulled
her own disappearing act, vanishing to America’s

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:35.520
west coast.

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:39.970
In Iceland, a rumor that Snowden was headed
there caused mass pro-Snowden protests on

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:42.750
the streets and a diplomatic incident with
the US.

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:46.750
In the Ecuadorian embassy in London, Julian
Assange leapt on the phone and demanded Wikileaks

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:49.770
get a representative to Hong Kong and co-opt
the story.

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:54.010
Which is how an eager young Brit named Sarah
Harrison wound up flying to the city with

00:20:54.010 --> 00:20:56.510
a mission to track down the NSA leaker.

00:20:56.510 --> 00:21:01.010
In Hong Kong itself, a gang of human rights
lawyers led by Canadian Robert Tibbo set up

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:04.780
a safe house system, giving Snowden places
to stay in the city’s slums.

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:09.010
As journalists and intelligence operatives
combed the seedy streets and waded through

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:14.030
pools of neon, Snowden disappeared into the
homes of Hong Kong’s forgotten and neglected.

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:19.420
Today, those that helped him – all asylum
seekers – are known as the Snowden refugees.

00:21:19.420 --> 00:21:22.799
Even without its main player, the story rumbled
on.

00:21:22.799 --> 00:21:27.150
On June 14, the US Justice Department charged
the missing contractor with espionage.

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:29.530
An arrest warrant was sent to the Hong Kong
authorities.

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:33.570
One week later, Robert Tibbo made contact
with Hong Kong officials, asking if they would

00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:34.929
extradite his client.

00:21:34.929 --> 00:21:38.110
By now, Wikileaks’ Sarah Harrison had successfully
found Snowden.

00:21:38.110 --> 00:21:42.330
When Hong Kong’s people declined to answer,
she suggested he head for asylum in Ecuador.

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:44.100
The net was closing in.

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:48.400
In an interview with Glenn Greenwald, CNN
had helpfully revealed that Snowden was still

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:49.400
in Hong Kong.

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:52.210
On June 22, the US authorities cancelled his
passport.

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:54.770
It was time for Snowden to run.

00:21:54.770 --> 00:21:58.850
The next day, Snowden and Harrison boarded
a flight to Moscow, supposedly intending to

00:21:58.850 --> 00:22:01.840
catch a flight to Havana, and then another
onto Ecuador.

00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:04.800
Hong Kong’s passport control failed to detain
either of them.

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:08.590
When asked why, they would later say the US
had filed an international arrest warrant

00:22:08.590 --> 00:22:12.010
for Edward James Snowden, not Edward Joseph
Snowden.

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:17.920
That evening, June 23, 2013, Snowden and Harrison
touched down at Moscow’s Sheremetyevo International

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:19.170
to a media circus.

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:23.240
Someone, likely an official in Hong Kong,
had leaked the airliner’s destination to

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:24.240
the press.

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:28.640
Under international pressure, Russian officials
refused to let Snowden board his Havana flight.

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:33.010
At the time, Vladimir Putin claimed Snowden’s
arrival in Moscow was “an unwanted Christmas

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:34.010
gift”.

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:37.370
However, the Wall Street Journal has since
claimed Snowden was in contact with the Kremlin

00:22:37.370 --> 00:22:40.990
in the last desperate days before his flight
from Hong Kong.

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:45.429
Regardless, it was here that Snowden’s time
as James Bond finally ended.

00:22:45.429 --> 00:22:49.790
Stuck in the international transit lounge
of the airport for a month, his entire life

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:51.000
was placed in limbo.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:55.730
Even as The Guardian and Washington Post kept
publishing stories based on his material,

00:22:55.730 --> 00:22:58.390
Snowden himself was regulated to a bit part.

00:22:58.390 --> 00:23:01.530
A strange ghost hanging around an airport
lounge.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:06.890
On August 1, 2013, over a month after he touched
down, Edward Snowden was allowed to leave

00:23:06.890 --> 00:23:08.090
Sheremetyevo airport with Harrison.

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:11.320
The whistleblower was granted temporary refugee
status in Russia.

00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:12.870
He’s been there ever since.

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:13.980
But don’t worry.

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:18.630
This video isn’t going to end with Snowden
slinking out a Moscow airport one warm day

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:19.750
in 2013.

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:28.570
There’s still one more act to get through.

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:31.100
The Aftermath
The legacy of Edward Snowden is a story in

00:23:31.100 --> 00:23:35.530
its own right, not least in how different
countries treated the revelations.

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:40.820
In the UK, agents from GCHQ raided the Guardian’s
offices and smashed up their hard drives.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:45.530
Not long after, Parliament passed new laws
actually expanding the agency’s powers.

00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:49.630
In the US, President Obama led the charge
personally criticizing Snowden, but nonetheless

00:23:49.630 --> 00:23:53.290
set up an independent panel examining US surveillance
practices.

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:59.559
In December 2013, the panel recommended that
bulk collection of data be suspended as unconstitutional.

00:23:59.559 --> 00:24:04.750
Today, the NSA operates under close Congressional
oversight.

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:05.750
What else?

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:09.710
Well, the Snowden revelations arguably led
to the EU’s extremely strict GDPR data protection

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:14.980
laws, just as they led to Russia, China and
others passing data sovereignty laws, meaning

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:20.380
any data collected on their citizens must
be physically stored on servers in their territory.

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:22.000
As for Greenwald and co?

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:24.840
They wound up winning the 2014 Pulitzer Prize.

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:29.390
On the darker side, US intelligence agencies
have said the damage done by the leaks was

00:24:29.390 --> 00:24:30.540
irreparable.

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:36.169
Trust was lost between nations, and terrorists
were given intimate knowledge of NSA operations.

00:24:36.169 --> 00:24:39.090
As for the man behind this chaos, well, it’s
been a mixed bag.

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:43.920
On October 9, 2013, Edward Snowden was spotted
in Moscow for the first time since leaving

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:44.920
the airport.

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:47.380
Not long after, his father Lon flew out to
visit.

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:50.420
Quite what the two talked about that day remains
a mystery.

00:24:50.420 --> 00:24:54.980
In March 2014, Snowden made his first online
speaking appearance at Texas’s South by

00:24:54.980 --> 00:24:56.200
Southwest festival.

00:24:56.200 --> 00:25:00.740
Today, such engagements are his main source
of income, netting him an estimated $200,000

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:01.740
a year.

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:05.140
Not that life is all roses and honey for the
man trapped in Moscow.

00:25:05.140 --> 00:25:10.950
In 2018, a despondent Snowden told reporters
that Russia was a corrupt society and he no

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:12.320
longer felt safe there.

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:16.309
As for the US, Washington still considers
the whistleblower a wanted man.

00:25:16.309 --> 00:25:21.000
So, at the end of all that excitement, what
can we really say about Edward Snowden?

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:22.510
Was he a hero or a traitor?

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:23.990
The answer is… who knows?

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:26.320
Yep, you didn’t really expect us to answer
that question, did you?

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:27.370
We’re just a regular ol’ YouTube channel.

00:25:27.370 --> 00:25:31.590
Despite what you’ll hear online, it’s
likely no-one knows the answer except for

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:33.080
Edward Snowden himself.

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:35.320
The story of Edward Snowden is not over yet.

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:38.960
It could end with an unexpected Presidential
pardon a week from now.

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:44.890
It could end in sixty years when Snowden dies
alone in an anonymous Moscow apartment, still

00:25:44.890 --> 00:25:46.179
a wanted man.

00:25:46.179 --> 00:25:48.750
Or it could end in a billion other ways.

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:53.220
All we know for sure is that the final chapter
in the life of Edward Snowden is still to

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:54.220
be written.

00:25:54.220 --> 00:26:08.230
But his place in history

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:22.789
is already assured.

